1d:T481,| Reported problem | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| The step or transition has no upstream or downstream element | unconnected element | draw the missing link |
| The transition has no expression, or its expression is invalid | empty receptivity or syntax error, for example `cycle_start AND` | fix the expression |
| Unknown variable | variable not declared in the variable table | declare it or fix its name |
| Step(s) with no path back to a cycle | last step with no outgoing transition or return link: the GRAFCET gets stuck there | add a transition that loops back |
| Step(s) unreachable from the initial step | step not linked to the sequence starting at the initial step | link it to the sequence |
| Several downstream elements after a step or a transition | branching without a junction | use an OR divergence (step) or an AND divergence (transition) |
| Receptivities not mutually exclusive | two branches of an OR divergence with identical conditions, or one always true | write exclusive conditions |
| Inconsistent AND convergence or divergence | unpaired branches, or divergence not closed | check the number of branches and the closing |7:["$","$L17",null,{"maxWidth":"lg","sx":{"my":4},"children":["$L18",["$","$L19",null,{"variant":"h2","component":"p","color":"primary","gutterBottom":true,"children":"Module A1: GRAFCET basics"}],false,["$","$L1a",null,{"sx":{"my":2}}],["$","$L1b",null,{"children":[false,["$","$L1c",null,{"steps":[{"id":"grafcet-definition","kind":"theory","title":"What is a GRAFCET?","body":["GRAFCET (the French acronym for \"step/transition function chart\", an international standard: IEC 60848) is a graphical language that describes what an automated system must do, step by step: what to do, in what order, under what conditions. It doesn't say what equipment to build it with: a PLC (seen in module A0) or relays (electromechanical switches) can run it. Its two building blocks are the step and the transition.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/exemple-barriere-en.svg\" width=\"340\" alt=\"GRAFCET of the barrier: initial step 0, the car_detected transition, step 1 with the lift_barrier action, the car_passed transition, then back to step 0\" /></figure></div>","By the end of this module, you will be able to:\n\n- read a GRAFCET;\n- write one, with timers;\n- branch sequences with OR and AND.\n\nThree theory steps, interspersed with short simulation observations, come before the first exercise.","Studomate lets you write a GRAFCET, simulate it (as in module A0) and analyse it: the Analyse button flags construction errors."]},{"id":"theory","kind":"theory","title":"Steps, transitions, receptivities, actions","body":["A **step** is a numbered square, active or inactive. Each step n exposes its own boolean variable Xn, true while it is active: X3 is TRUE when step 3 is active. The number is free but unique, and doesn't have to be consecutive. Usually only one step is active at a time; AND divergences, further down, are the exception.","The **initial step** (double outline) is the one active at power-on or after a reset. A GRAFCET always has at least one.","While a step is active, its **actions** run: for example, turning on a boolean output such as `output1`. An action is a rectangle attached to the step.","A **transition** (a small horizontal bar) links two steps and carries a **receptivity** (the transition condition), that is the crossing condition: a true-or-false expression, such as `end1` or `cycle_start AND NOT stop`.","Steps and transitions are joined by **directed links**: plain vertical lines, read from top to bottom. An arrow is only drawn when going back up, to loop to a step higher up.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/grafcet-symbols-en.svg\" width=\"380\" alt=\"GRAFCET symbols: inactive step, active step, initial step with a double outline, transition with its receptivity, action attached to a step, directed link with a return arrow\" /></figure></div>","A GRAFCET moves forward by **evolution**: as soon as the upstream step is active and the receptivity of the transition that follows it becomes true, the transition is crossed, the upstream step deactivates and the downstream step activates. For example, when `end1` becomes true while step 1 is active, step 2 takes over.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/evolution-3-temps-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Evolution in three stages: step 1 is active and end1 is false, then end1 becomes true and the transition is crossed, then step 2 is active\" /></figure></div>","Steps and transitions always alternate: a step is always followed by a transition, and a transition by a step. To go in several directions, you have to use a divergence, presented further down.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/alternance-etape-transition-en.svg\" width=\"420\" alt=\"Strict alternation: step, transition, step, transition is allowed; two steps in a row or two transitions in a row are forbidden\" /></figure></div>"]},{"id":"observe-linear","kind":"exercise","title":"Watch: steps and transitions in action","body":["Before drawing a GRAFCET, watch one run: the linear sequence solution opens directly in simulation. Reminder: each numbered step `n` has a variable `Xn`, true while the step is active (`X0` for step 0, `X1` for step 1...).","Step by step:\n\n1. Click \"Watch the simulation\": the simulation starts automatically.\n2. In the GRAFCET, spot the active step (highlighted): it is the initial step.\n3. In the watch table, hold `cycle_start` true: the next step becomes active and `output1` turns on.\n4. Set `end1` true, then `end2`: follow the active step and the outputs.","**Check question**: Which variable is TRUE when step 1 is active?\n\n<details><summary>Show the answer</summary>\n\n<code>X1</code>, the variable of that step.\n\n</details>"],"embeddedTemplateId":"linear-sequence","embeddedWatchOpen":true,"cta":{"templateId":"linear-sequence","exerciseLabel":"Watch the simulation","primaryMode":"solution","autostartSimulation":true}},{"id":"theory-actions","kind":"theory","title":"Action types and execution modes","body":["An action runs when its step is active. It acts on a boolean variable (for example `output1`, to turn on an output) or a numeric one (for example the assignment `spaces := spaces + 1`, where `:=` reads \"receives\").","The execution mode defines when the action runs. An edge is the exact instant a signal changes: rising (from false to true), falling (from true to false). Here, it is the step's edge: rising when it activates, falling when it deactivates. The available modes depend on the variable's type:","| Mode | When the action runs | Used for | Variable |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Continuous | every scan, while the step is active | turning on an output, recomputing a value | boolean, numeric |\n| Rising edge | just once, when the step activates | incrementing a counter just once | numeric |\n| Falling edge | just once, when the step deactivates | acting at the end of a phase | numeric |\n| Set | forces the variable true, which stays true when the step deactivates (a \"stored\" action) | starting a motor and leaving it running | boolean |\n| Reset | forces the variable false | stopping what Set started | boolean |","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/action-modes-chronogram-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Execution modes timing diagram: a continuous action follows the step, a rising-edge action runs for one scan on activation, a falling-edge action for one scan on deactivation\" /></figure></div>","With a 100 ms scan cycle (the simulator's default setting), a step active for 3.4 s runs its action 34 times in continuous mode, but only once on an edge. To increment a counter just once, you therefore use an edge mode.","A Set action stays true when the step deactivates, until a Reset action sets it back to false: that is what lets you start a motor in one step and stop it in another.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/action-set-reset-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Set and Reset timing diagram: the motor variable turns true with step 1 and stays true until step 4, whereas a continuous action drops with step 1\" /></figure></div>"]},{"id":"observe-actions","kind":"exercise","title":"Watch: an action on an edge","body":["The car park solution increments a space counter with a rising-edge action. See what that changes. Reminder: a Continuous-mode action is recomputed on every cycle while the step is active, whereas a rising-edge action runs only once, on the cycle when the step becomes active.","Step by step:\n\n1. Click \"Watch the simulation\": the simulation starts automatically.\n2. Open the \"3D scene\" tab and click \"Arrival\": a car shows up and the barrier opens.\n3. In the scene's console, watch \"Occupied spaces\" (the `spaces` variable): it goes up by 1, just once per car, even if the step stays active for several scans.","**Check question**: What would <code>spaces</code> do if the <code>spaces := spaces + 1</code> action were in Continuous mode?\n\n<details><summary>Show the answer</summary>\n\nit would go up on every scan, as long as the step is active.\n\n</details>"],"embeddedTemplateId":"parking","embeddedScene3d":true,"cta":{"templateId":"parking","exerciseLabel":"Watch the simulation","primaryMode":"solution","autostartSimulation":true}},{"id":"exercise-linear","kind":"exercise","title":"Exercise: linear sequence","body":["Write the GRAFCET of a simple cycle: one output, then the other, then everything switches off and the cycle can start again. There is no divergence and no timer. To get used to the editor (adding a step, drawing a transition), the user manual (Help menu) will guide you. Open the analysis panel (Analyse button) every time you add a transition: reading the messages is covered at the end of the module.","Inputs: `cycle_start` (cycle start, a push button), `end1` (limit switch 1) and `end2` (limit switch 2). Outputs: `output1` and `output2`.\n\nWork to do:\n\n1. On `cycle_start`, turn on `output1`.\n2. On `end1`, turn off `output1` and turn on `output2`.\n3. On `end2`, turn off `output2`.\n4. On a new press of `cycle_start`, restart the cycle from the beginning.\n\nA continuous action switches itself off when its step deactivates: there is no need to deactivate it explicitly.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/linear-sequence-chronogram-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Expected timing diagram: output1 turns on after cycle_start and drops when end1 arrives, output2 takes over until end2\" /></figure></div>","How to tell it is right: in simulation, after `cycle_start` alone `output1` is true; after `end1`, `output1` drops and `output2` turns on; after `end2` everything is off; a new press of `cycle_start` restarts the cycle. The analysis panel reports no error.","<details><summary>Hints</summary><ol><li>How many different states does the system go through during a cycle, rest included?</li><li>Which step must be active at power-on?</li><li>How do you get back to the initial step at the end of the cycle? The solution uses 4 steps, but other solutions are valid.</li></ol></details>"],"embeddedTemplateId":"linear-sequence","embeddedExercise":{"exerciseTab":"Exercise","solutionTab":"Solution","initialView":{"type":"grafcet"}},"embeddedWatchOpen":true,"cta":{"templateId":"linear-sequence","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor"}},{"id":"theory-timer","kind":"theory","title":"Timing a receptivity","body":["A traffic light stays green for 5 seconds, a stairwell light goes off after a delay: in both cases, the transition must become true a certain time after entering the step. A receptivity (transition condition) can therefore depend on the time spent in a step, not just on an input.","In Studomate, it is written `t1/X10/5s`:\n\n- `t1` is the name of the timer: a free name, unique in the grafcet and different from all your variables;\n- `X10` means the time counts while step 10 is active;\n- `5s` is the duration: the possible units are `ms`, `s`, `m`, `h` and `d` (for example `500ms` or `2m`).","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/timer-notation-en.svg\" width=\"340\" alt=\"Anatomy of t1/X10/5s: t1 is the name of the timer, X10 the step whose activity makes the time count, 5s the duration\" /></figure></div>","The time is measured from the instant `X10` becomes true. When the 5 seconds have elapsed, `t1/X10/5s` becomes true and the transition can be crossed. If the step is left before the end, the time restarts from zero at the next activation.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/timer-chronogram-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Timing diagram: X10 is true for 5 seconds, the elapsed time rises to 5 s, t1/X10/5s becomes true at 5 s, then X11 activates\" /></figure></div>","This expression combines like any boolean condition: `cycle_start AND t1/X10/5s` (the button AND the elapsed delay), or `end1 OR t1/X10/10s` (the limit switch, or failing that a safety after 10 seconds).","<details><summary>Going further</summary>You will meet other notations in other manuals: the standard (IEC 60848) writes <code>5s/X10</code> for a 5-second delay after step 10 activates, and the old French notation (NF C 03-190) writes <code>T/X10/5s</code>. Studomate's syntax is close to the latter.</details>"]},{"id":"observe-timer","kind":"exercise","title":"Watch: a timer","body":["The solution of this small two-step GRAFCET uses timed receptivities: watch them at work. There is no button: time alone moves the cycle forward.","Step by step:\n\n1. Click \"Watch the simulation\": the simulation starts automatically.\n2. Spot the active step (highlighted) and, in the watch table, the output that is TRUE.\n3. Time it: next to each transition, the elapsed time is displayed. The step stays active until its timer elapses, then the other one takes over.\n4. In the GRAFCET, find the text written next to each transition: it is its receptivity.","**Check question**: For how long is `output2` true on each round?\n\n<details><summary>Show the answer</summary>\n\n2 seconds: it is true as long as step 1 is active, and <code>t1/X1/2s</code> becomes true 2 seconds after that step activates.\n\n</details>"],"embeddedTemplateId":"timer-cycle","cta":{"templateId":"timer-cycle","exerciseLabel":"Watch the simulation","primaryMode":"solution","autostartSimulation":true}},{"id":"synthesis-traffic-light","kind":"synthesis","title":"Summary assignment: traffic light","body":["This assignment introduces nothing new: it combines steps, transitions, actions and timers. Write the GRAFCET of a traffic light that loops through its three colours: one step per colour, three timers (green 5 s, amber 2 s, red 5 s) and a loop back. The outputs are `green`, `amber` and `red`. The HMI (human-machine interface) is the supervision screen: the project provided already contains one, with the three indicators.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/traffic-light-chronogram-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Expected timing diagram: green for 5 seconds, amber for 2 seconds, red for 5 seconds, only one colour active at a time\" /></figure></div>","How to tell it is right: in simulation, only one indicator is lit at a time; the green, amber, red cycle loops without any button press; the analysis panel reports no error.","<details><summary>Hints</summary><ol><li>How many steps do you need for three colours?</li><li>For the green duration, the receptivity is written <code>t0/X0/5s</code>: adapt the name, the step and the duration for the other two colours.</li><li>To loop, the last transition goes back to the initial step: that is a return link, as in the barrier GRAFCET.</li></ol></details>"],"embeddedTemplateId":"traffic-light","embeddedExercise":{"exerciseTab":"Exercise","solutionTab":"Solution","initialView":{"type":"grafcet"}},"cta":{"templateId":"traffic-light","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor"}},{"id":"theory-or-divergence","kind":"theory","title":"OR divergence / convergence","body":["An **OR divergence** is a T junction: after a step, you go left OR right, never both. Each **branch** is a possible sequence, and it carries its own receptivity (transition condition), placed after the **bar** (the horizontal line, single for OR). It is a choice: if `cycle_start1`, branch 1; if `cycle_start2`, branch 2.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/or-divergence-en.svg\" width=\"300\" alt=\"OR divergence: step 0, a single bar, then two branches, each with its transition (cycle_start1 or cycle_start2) then its step; only one path is taken\" /></figure></div>","At any instant, only one receptivity among the branches should be true: the branches are said to be mutually exclusive, because they cannot be crossed together. If `cycle_start1` and `cycle_start2` are true at the same time, both branches are crossed and two steps activate: the result is inconsistent.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/or-exclusivity-en.svg\" width=\"460\" alt=\"Why exclusivity: with cycle_start1 and cycle_start2 true together, two steps activate; with cycle_start1 AND NOT cycle_start2 and cycle_start2 AND NOT cycle_start1, no branch is crossed in that case\" /></figure></div>","Studomate only flags the obvious cases (two identical receptivities, or one that is always true). It is up to you to guarantee exclusivity: inputs that cannot be true together, or conditions written like `cycle_start1 AND NOT cycle_start2`.","The branches can then rejoin further down on a common sequence through an **OR convergence**, but this is not mandatory: each branch can also loop back on its own to the initial step, as in the next exercise.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/or-convergence-variants-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Two ways to end an OR divergence: an OR convergence to a common step, or each branch looping back to step 0\" /></figure></div>"]},{"id":"exercise-or-divergence","kind":"exercise","title":"Exercise: OR divergence","body":["Write the GRAFCET of a choice between two sequences: depending on the button pressed, one output or the other turns on, then the system returns to the initial state.","Inputs: `cycle_start1` and `cycle_start2` (two push buttons), `end1` and `end2` (limit switches). Outputs: `output1` and `output2`.\n\nWork to do:\n\n1. On `cycle_start1`, turn on `output1`; on `end1`, return to the initial state.\n2. On `cycle_start2` (exclusive of `cycle_start1`), turn on `output2`; on `end2`, return to the initial state.\n3. Give the same receptivity to both branches and read the analysis panel's message, then restore exclusive receptivities.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/or-divergence-chronogram-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Expected timing diagram: a cycle started by cycle_start1 turns on output1 until end1, then a cycle started by cycle_start2 turns on output2 until end2, never at the same time\" /></figure></div>","How to tell it is right: after `cycle_start1`, `output1` turns on alone until `end1`, then everything turns off; after `cycle_start2`, `output2` turns on alone until `end2`. With exclusive receptivities, the analysis panel reports no error.","<details><summary>Question: what happens if you press cycle_start1 and cycle_start2 at the same time?</summary>With the receptivities <code>cycle_start1</code> and <code>cycle_start2</code>, both branches are crossed and both outputs turn on together. To prevent this, write <code>cycle_start1 AND NOT cycle_start2</code> and <code>cycle_start2 AND NOT cycle_start1</code>: no branch is crossed while both buttons are pressed.</details>","<details><summary>Hints</summary><ol><li>How many steps do you need: the initial step, plus one per branch?</li><li>Which step does each branch loop back to?</li><li>Test by pressing <code>cycle_start1</code>, then <code>cycle_start2</code>.</li></ol></details>","The next step presents the AND divergence. You will then reuse the OR choice in the \"barrier car park\" summary assignment."],"embeddedTemplateId":"or-divergence","embeddedExercise":{"exerciseTab":"Exercise","solutionTab":"Solution","initialView":{"type":"grafcet"}},"cta":{"templateId":"or-divergence","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor"}},{"id":"theory-and-divergence","kind":"theory","title":"AND divergence / convergence","body":["An **AND divergence** starts several branches in parallel: when a single transition is crossed, several steps activate at the same time. It is drawn with a **double bar** (two horizontal lines), and its receptivity (transition condition) is placed **before** the bar: one condition alone triggers all the branches.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/and-divergence-en.svg\" width=\"300\" alt=\"AND divergence: step 0, the cycle_start transition, a double bar, then two branches whose steps 1 and 2 are active at the same time\" /></figure></div>","Unlike an OR divergence, where each branch carries its own receptivity after the single bar and only one path is taken, an AND divergence starts all the paths at once: OR is a choice, AND is parallelism.","The **AND convergence** closes the divergence: the rest of the GRAFCET only continues once every branch has reached its last step, the one before the convergence bar. The slowest branch sets the pace for the others.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/and-convergence-sync-en.svg\" width=\"480\" alt=\"AND convergence: branch 1 is ready at 2 seconds, branch 2 at 5 seconds, and the sequence only continues at 5 seconds\" /></figure></div>","Studomate flags an error for an AND convergence that doesn't come from an AND divergence: reading analysis errors is covered further down in this module.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/or-vs-and-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Comparison: with OR only one path is taken, with AND all the paths are started at the same time\" /></figure></div>"]},{"id":"exercise-and-divergence","kind":"exercise","title":"Exercise: AND divergence","body":["Write the GRAFCET of a cycle start that launches two actions in parallel, then waits for both to finish.","Inputs: `cycle_start` (cycle start), `sensor1` and `sensor2`. Outputs: `output1` and `output2`.\n\nWork to do:\n\n1. On `cycle_start`, turn on `output1` and `output2` **simultaneously**.\n2. Wait for `sensor1` **and** `sensor2` to be active before returning to the initial state (both outputs turn off).","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/and-divergence-chronogram-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Expected timing diagram: cycle_start turns on output1 and output2 together, they only turn off once sensor1 and sensor2 are both true\" /></figure></div>","How to tell it is right: after `cycle_start`, both outputs are true together; in simulation, activate `sensor1` alone: nothing happens; then activate `sensor2`: both outputs turn off, whatever the order of the sensors. The analysis panel reports no error.","<details><summary>Hints</summary><ol><li>How many parallel branches do you need, and which bar opens and closes them?</li><li>A single transition, placed after the convergence bar, can combine both sensors.</li><li>Test in the other order: <code>sensor2</code> first, then <code>sensor1</code>.</li></ol></details>"],"embeddedTemplateId":"and-divergence","embeddedExercise":{"exerciseTab":"Exercise","solutionTab":"Solution","initialView":{"type":"grafcet"}},"cta":{"templateId":"and-divergence","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor"}},{"id":"synthesis-parking","kind":"synthesis","title":"Summary assignment: barrier car park","body":["Write the control of a 4-space car park, with an entry barrier, an exit barrier and a \"full\" indicator. This assignment reuses the OR choice, but also the numeric actions and the compound conditions seen earlier.","Inputs: `entry_request` and `exit_request` (a car is waiting in front of the entry or exit barrier) and `car_present` (cell under a barrier, true while a car is on it). Outputs: `entry_barrier`, `exit_barrier` (opening) and `full` (indicator). The number of occupied spaces is tracked in `spaces`.\n\nThe car park is visible in 3D (View menu, 3D scene): the \"Arrival\" and \"Departure\" buttons make cars appear, and they only move forward if their barrier is open.\n\nWork to do:\n\n1. On `entry_request`, if the car park is not full: open the entry barrier, increment `spaces`.\n2. On `exit_request`, if the car park is not empty: open the exit barrier, decrement `spaces`.\n3. Turn on `full` as soon as all the spaces are taken and refuse new entries.\n4. Close the barrier once the car has passed (`car_present`), then validate in simulation with the 3D scene.","How to tell it is right: with 4 arrivals in a row, `full` turns on and a 5th car stays blocked; after an exit, `full` turns off; `spaces` never goes below 0.","<details><summary>Hints</summary><ol><li>Two distinct sequences start from the waiting state: one for entry, one for exit. Which mechanism chooses one or the other?</li><li>Count about 5 steps in all: the waiting state and two steps per sequence.</li><li><code>spaces := spaces + 1</code> is a numeric action: for it to run only once per car, use the rising edge mode (see the step on actions).</li><li>The <code>full</code> indicator is driven in a second GRAFCET program, for example \"Signalisation\", that compares <code>spaces</code> to the car park capacity. The user manual explains how to add a grafcet to a project.</li></ol></details>"],"cta":{"templateId":"parking","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor","solutionLabel":"Solution"}},{"id":"synthesis-crossroads","kind":"synthesis","title":"Summary assignment: traffic light crossroads","body":["Write the GRAFCET of a junction with two axes of traffic lights: North-South (lights `NS1` and `NS2`) and East-West (lights `EW1` and `EW2`). The twelve outputs (`red…`, `amber…` and `green…` of each light, for example `greenNS1`) are already created in the project: in an action, pick the variable from the list.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/crossroads-layout-en.svg\" width=\"380\" alt=\"Junction plan seen from above: the North-South axis with lights NS1 and NS2, the East-West axis with lights EW1 and EW2\" /></figure></div>","A 3D scene (View menu, 3D scene) shows the junction: cars arrive continuously on all four branches and obey the lights. If both axes are green at the same time, or without enough clearance time, the cars collide: the collision is displayed and the accident counter goes up. The amber light matters: a car too close to brake goes through anyway, hence the **all-red** phase, a moment when every light is red to let the junction clear.","Work to do:\n\n1. The two lights of the same axis show the same colour at the same time: a single step can activate several actions.\n2. Alternate the two axes, with an all-red phase between each change.\n3. Time the phases (green 10 s, amber 2 s, all red 2 s) and validate the full cycle in simulation, checking in the 3D scene that no collision occurs.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/crossroads-phases-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Expected behaviour of the lights: the North-South axis is green for 10 s, amber for 2 s, then everything is red for 2 s; then the East-West axis is green for 10 s, amber for 2 s, then everything is red for 2 s\" /></figure></div>","How to tell it is right: only one axis is green at any time, amber appears before each all-red phase, and the accident counter stays at 0 over two full cycles.","<details><summary>Hints</summary><ol><li>How many distinct phases does a full cycle have?</li><li>A phase can correspond to one step, with one action per lit output.</li></ol></details>"],"cta":{"templateId":"crossroads","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor","solutionLabel":"Solution"}},{"id":"reading-errors","kind":"theory","title":"Reading analysis errors","body":["Studomate analyses your GRAFCET without waiting for a simulation: a click on \"Analyse\" opens the analysis panel, which lists the problems found. Get in the habit of opening it every time you add a transition or a branch: that is where most errors appear. The \"Analyse\" button turns red when there are errors; a click on a line of the panel selects the faulty element in the GRAFCET.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/analysis-panel-en.webp\" width=\"560\" alt=\"Studomate &quot;Analysis results&quot; panel: under Errors, the GRAFCET &quot;Carrefour de feux tricolores&quot; reports &quot;[Action] Unknown variable: vertNS1p&quot;; the Warnings section says &quot;No warning&quot;.\" /></figure></div>","$1d","The two easiest defects to miss are a step with no way back, which blocks the GRAFCET forever, and a step never reached from the initial step, which will never activate. They often appear when a sequence is not looped back to its starting point.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/grafcet/analysis-dead-end-unreachable-en.svg\" width=\"440\" alt=\"Two defects: (a) a last step with no outgoing transition, reported as having no path back to a cycle; (b) two steps not linked to the initial step, reported as unreachable\" /></figure></div>","What you can do now:\n\n- read a GRAFCET;\n- write a sequence of steps and transitions;\n- time a receptivity;\n- choose a path with an OR divergence;\n- start parallel branches with an AND divergence;\n- read the analysis messages.","The rest of the path covers Ladder (modules A2 to A5): you will learn to program a PLC, up to translating a GRAFCET into a program."]}],"prevLabel":"Previous","nextLabel":"Next","moduleId":"a1","initialStepId":"theory-and-divergence","nextModule":{"href":"/training/a2","label":"Move on to module A2"}}]]}],"$L1e","$L1f"]}]
